<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  =====================================================================
  ctxroute-adapter - reconcile the dedicated loopback adapter at BOOT.
  =====================================================================

  WHY A SECOND TASK AND NOT AN ACTION OF THE DAEMON'S TASK.
    PRIVILEGE SEPARATION. Creating a network adapter needs elevation; serving
    hook frames does not. The daemon therefore stays an ordinary user process
    and this task - which runs as SYSTEM - does ONE thing and exits. Merging
    them would give the long-lived process privileges it never needs, for the
    lifetime of the session.

  WHY BOOT AND NOT LOGON.
    The daemon's task is triggered at LOGON. This one must have finished
    BEFORE it, or the daemon would try to bind an address that does not exist
    yet. Boot precedes every logon, which is what orders the two without any
    coordination between them.

  WHY SYSTEM AND NOT "the administrator".
    S-1-5-18 is always available, needs no stored password, and never raises a
    UAC prompt. That is what makes elevation a ONE-TIME cost, paid when the
    task is registered - never again at every boot.

  WHAT IT IS NOT.
    Not a watchdog, not a heartbeat, not a health check. It converges towards a
    declared state and exits; if the state is already correct it changes
    nothing and says so. Re-running it is always safe.

  DECLARED LIMIT, written rather than hidden: this runs at BOOT ONLY. An
  adapter removed while the machine is up is NOT repaired until the next boot.
  It is DETECTED at the next session start by the doctor's wiring check, which
  names the dead address and the command that repairs it. Continuous
  reconciliation was refused on purpose: a privileged task waking on a timer,
  for ever, to fix something that has never yet happened, is a cost with no
  measurement behind it.

  (No long option is ever spelled inside these comments: XML forbids a double
  hyphen anywhere in a comment, and a real parser rejects the whole file. That
  is what happened here on the very first run of the installer, and what the
  service units gate catches by reading every unit in this directory.)

  ExecutionTimeLimit is BOUNDED here, unlike the daemon's task (PT0S, "run
  indefinitely"): this action is a short convergence, and a reconciler that
  hangs must be stopped rather than held open.
-->
<Task version="1.4" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>Reconciles the dedicated loopback adapter that the ctxroute HTTP lane binds, so the declared listening address exists before the daemon starts.</Description>
    <URI>\ctxroute-adapter</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <BootTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </BootTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>S-1-5-18</UserId>
      <RunLevel>HighestAvailable</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <AllowHardTerminate>true</AllowHardTerminate>
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>
    <RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>false</RunOnlyIfNetworkAvailable>
    <AllowStartOnDemand>true</AllowStartOnDemand>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <Hidden>false</Hidden>
    <RunOnlyIfIdle>false</RunOnlyIfIdle>
    <WakeToRun>false</WakeToRun>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT5M</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <Priority>7</Priority>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>CHANGE_ME_COMMAND</Command>
      <Arguments>CHANGE_ME_ARGUMENTS</Arguments>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
